A new dimension-free theorem was proved and pushed.

The multistage direct-gluing identity gives
\[
\frac OT\ge 1-\frac{gp}{(g+h)(p+g-s)}
\]
when:

- Lmax has the specified two-block structure;
- the Lmin payoffs on \(G\) lie above those on \(P\);
- an Lmin-tight coalition \(H\cup J\) exists with \(s=|J|\le p\).

Unlike the earlier two-stratum observation, Lmin may now have arbitrarily many payoff levels. An explicit residual \(R_x\) captures the order currency, and the proof shows \(R_x\ge0\) by averaging. Equality conditions are also characterized.

Evidence includes:

- 1,080 exact two-stratum arithmetic checks;
- 680 nonuniform multistage checks, including positive slack cases;
- an independent symbolic simplification of both identities;
- a fresh exact three-generator \(n=7\) control;
- 14 exact staged-selection certificates;
- exact rooted branch minima;
- deterministic 45-page paper and arXiv builds.

This is substantial structural progress, but not yet the all-\(n\) breakthrough. The two remaining universal steps are:

1. Force a suitable coalition \(H\cup J\) from the complete staged Lmin packet.
2. Extend the argument from a two-level Lmax block to arbitrary nested Lmax strata—most plausibly by threshold-layer decomposition and coherent gluing across layers.

