Two fully human lemmas have now been extracted from the hardest \((4,2,0)\) machine residue.

- Class 8 closes with one exchange. Its activation follows from \(D>1/3\), and all 20 eligible blockers fail the opposite-allocation comparison. No \(q^1\) inequality is used.
- Class 3 closes through a two-exchange relay. The first exchange has exactly two possible blockers; either activates the second exchange; it again has exactly two blockers; and all four terminal blocker pairs imply
  \[
  D\le x_1\le \frac13,
  \]
  contradicting the strict-counterexample premise.

Together these lemmas remove six of the 32 feasible labelled packets from machine dependence. The remaining residue is now nine symmetry classes covering 26 labelled packets.

This is meaningful structural progress: the computer proof is beginning to yield an intelligible exchange mechanism, rather than merely a shorter enumeration. It does not change the theorem statement or establish field-level novelty.

